   "source": [
    "# Healthcare Analytics for Doctor Visits\n",
    
    "## Why this matters?\n",
    "Some people see a doctor again and again, while others hardly ever go. Hospitals, clinics and insurers all want to know why, because it helps them plan staff, appointments and support. In this project I use a real survey dataset to find out which things go along with more doctor visits.\n",
    "\n",
    "## The question\n",
    "**Why do some people visit a doctor more often than others?**\n",
    "\n",
    "I break it into three smaller questions:\n",
    "1. Do people who are sicker visit more?\n",
    "2. Do age and gender make a difference?\n",
    "3. Does the type of health insurance matter?\n",
    "\n",
    "## About the data\n",
    "- **5,190 adults** from an Australian health survey (1977–78)\n",
    "- **13 columns**, which I group like this:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Group | Columns | Meaning |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Doctor visits | `visits` | Doctor visits in the past 2 weeks |\n",
    "| About the person | `gender`, `age`, `income` | Basic details (age and income are stored scaled, I fix that in Step 2) |\n",
    "| Health | `illness`, `reduced`, `health`, `nchronic`, `lchronic` | Illnesses, days of reduced activity, a health score (higher = worse), chronic conditions |\n",
    "| Insurance | `private`, `freepoor`, `freerepat` | Private cover, or free government cover |\n",
    "\n",
    "## My plan\n",
    "1. Load and check the data\n",
    "2. Clean it\n",
    "3. Look at the big picture\n",
    "4. Compare groups with simple bar charts\n",
    "5. Write down what I found, and what I should be careful about\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to read the charts:** most people made zero visits, so I compare the **% of people who visited at least once** in each group. A higher bar means that group goes to the doctor more."
   ]
  },
  {
