   "source": [
    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "**Short answer:** people visit a doctor more when they are *sicker* and *older*. Money and the type of insurance mattered much less.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What I found\n",
    "1. **Most people did not visit at all.** Only about 20% (1 in 5) saw a doctor in the 2 weeks, and the average was 0.3 visits per person.\n",
    "2. **Sickness is the biggest factor.** 42% of people with 5 illnesses visited, against 7% with none. People with 8–14 days of reduced activity visited at 64%, against 15% for 0 days. People with the worst health scores (6–12) visited at 44%, against 15% for the best.\n",
    "3. **Age and gender.** 30% of people aged 65+ visited, against 15% of 15–24 year olds. Women visited more than men (24% vs 16%).\n",
    "4. **Chronic conditions.** Having a chronic condition that limits activity doubled the visit rate (35% vs 18%).\n",
    "5. **Insurance.** Private insurance made no difference (20% vs 20%). People on free cover for old age, disability or veteran status visited more (32% vs 17%). People on free low-income cover visited *less* (9% vs 21%), which surprised me.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Points to note\n",
    "- These are **patterns, not proof of cause**. For example, sick people visit more, but the data can't tell me what else sits behind that.\n",
    "- Illness and visits are measured in the **same 2 weeks**, so the two are tied together.\n",
    "- Age and illness overlap (older people are often sicker), so I can't fully separate them with simple charts.\n",
    "- The low-income group has only **222 people**, so I wouldn't trust that result much.\n",
    "- The survey is from **1977–78** and had more young and old people than a typical sample, so it may not match today's population.\n",
    "\n",

   ]
